  "Number": 46,
  "Question": "The graphs of \\(2x+3y-6=0,\\) \\(4x-3y-6=0,\\) \\(x=2,\\) and \\(y=\\dfrac23\\) intersect in:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(6\\) points",
    "B": "\\(1\\) point",
    "C": "\\(2\\) points",
    "D": "no points",
    "E": "an unlimited number of points"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The lines \\(x=2\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac23\\) have exactly one common point, \\(\\left(2,\\tfrac23\\right)\\). Any point common to all four lines must therefore be this point. Substitution into the other equations gives\n\\[\n2(2)+3\\left(\\frac23\\right)-6=4+2-6=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n4(2)-3\\left(\\frac23\\right)-6=8-2-6=0.\n\\]\nThus this point lies on all four lines, and there can be no other common point. The answer is one point, choice B.",
